Run health checks in application space
Currently, health checks are registered at the server as follows.

```ts
import { startServer } from 'jitar';
import DatabaseHealthCheck from './health/DatabaseHealthCheck.js';

const server = await startServer(moduleImporter);
server.addHealthCheck('database', new DatabaseHealthCheck());
```

And activated in the service configuration like this:

```json
{
    "url": "http://127.0.0.1:3000",
    "healthChecks": ["database"],
    "standalone": {}
}
```

Although this works well for most checks, others can't perform their task. Health checks run in the service space, which is a separate space from the application space. Meaning that if, for example, the database gets connected from the application space, the database health check can't check the database health because it runs in the service space.

To solve this issue, we need to rethink the health check registration. The following solution is proposed.

Health checks are registered in the service configuration by importing modules containing a health check instance.

```json
{
    "url": "http://127.0.0.1:3000",
    "healthChecks": ["./databaseHealthCheck"],
    "standalone": {}
}
```

An example health check instance module looks like this.

```
// src/databaseHealthCheck.ts
import DatabaseHealthCheck from './health/DatabaseHealthCheck.js';

const instance = new DatabaseHealthCheck();
export default instance;
```

In this example the health check class and instance are separated. This is good practice to enable reusability of the health checks. For simple cases, the class and instance can be defined in the same module file.

This proposed solution also makes the bootstrapper file more generic by removing the registration from the server.

Use expression character class instead of lookaround
**Motivation**
Due to the lack of set operation for character classes, I wrote regexes like `(?!\s)[\w\P{ASCII}]` in the past ([another example](https://github.com/ota-meshi/eslint-plugin-regexp/issues/574#issuecomment-1711508301)). They can be transformed to use set operations instead. Using set operation not only makes the intent clearer, it's also more performant. I did some performance test a while back, and `(?!\s)[\w\P{ASCII}]` was about 4x slower than `[\w...whatever ranges are equivalent]`.

**Description**
Combine single-character lookarounds with character classes in regexes with the `v` flag. E.g. `(?=[x])[y]` => `[y&&x]` and `(?![x])[y]` => `[y--x]`. Regexes without the `v` should not be affected.

If the element after the lookaround is a character set, then the transformation should still be applied. E.g. `(?!\s)\P{ASCII}` => `[\P{ASCII}--\s]`.

**Examples**

```js
// good
/(?!\s)[\w\x80-\uFFFF]/;
/(?!\s)[\w\P{ASCII}]/u;

// bad
bad  = /(?!\s)[\w\P{ASCII}]/v;
good = /[[\w\P{ASCII}]--\s]/v;
```

Introduce basic support for push notifications mocking on Android
### Description

Define and implement an API for mocking push notification invocation events on Android, so as to introduce parity with the equivalent API for iOS.

This has long been a missing functionality on Android compared to iOS, as - unlike on iOS, there's no skeletal definition of notification invocation events. Rather, on Android, notifications fire pending intents, originally produced upon notification arrival handling *by the app itself*; All the while there's also no restriction as to how and when notifications show, and what the nature of the registered pending intent is.

The general direction is towards formalizing a reduced intents API, to be used by the JS test code.

#### Phase 1

In this basic-support phase, we limit the functionality to a naive one where the same intent is always used*, and the user is only able to provide payload, using the API's already defined for iOS.

This boils down to 3 types of use cases:

```js
    const userNotification = {
      payload: {
        userData1: 'userDataValue1',
        userData2: 'userDataValue2',
      },
    };

    // #1 - most common
    await device.launchApp({ newInstance: true, userNotification });

    // #2 - `launchApp` used for a running app (a not very appealing API overall)
    await device.launchApp({ newInstance: false, userNotification });

    // #3 - The dedicated function
    await device.launchApp({newInstance: true});
    await device.sendUserNotification(userNotification);
```
> Note: while `sendUserNotification()` is an API that's not very idiomatic on Android, we use it here nonetheless, assuming we will abandon all of this in phase 2 (see below).

#### Phase 2

For the full vision and more versatile Android-idiomatic solution, see #2141.

---
<sup> * This refers to a simply, launcher-like intent, with user payload "flat"-set in its extras -- much like the way the default FCM impl. handles *message* notifications on the device.</sup>

