Fire global events that could be subscribed to
### New feature motivation

I am writing an automated test suite that, in part, covers code which uses swal2 to display modals. I wanted to capture a list of all the times the modal is opened and closed across the tests... a list of `[ "show", "hide", "show", ... ]` if you will. That way I could compare such a list to the expected number of shows and hides.

Unfortunately, I don't see any events being fired, which I can subscribe to, that let me know when the modal is shown or hidden -- from the outside, as opposed to injecting event handler (`willShow`, etc) into each `.fire(..)` call.

Here's the sorta hack/work-around I came up with:

```js
var events = [];

var observer = new MutationObserver(mutationList => {
    events.push(
        ...(
            mutationList.filter(mutation => (
                mutation.type == "attributes" &&
                mutation.attributeName == "class" &&
                mutation.target.matches(".swal2-show, .swal2-hide")
            ))
            .map(mutation => (
                mutation.target.className.match(/\bswal2-(show|hide)\b/)[1]
            ))
        )
    );
});

observer.observe(document.body,{ attributes: true, subtree: true, });

// later, after the test finishes, remove consecutive duplicates
events = events.reduce((list,v) => (
    list.length == 0 || list[list.length - 1] != v ?
        [ ...list, v ] :
        list
),[]);

events;   // [ "show", "hide", "show", ... ]
```

This seems to work for my usage, but... is far from ideal (especially the mutation observer part).

### New feature description

I'd love it if I could avoid the `MutationObserver`, and instead the `Swal` object was an event emitter (like `EventEmitter3`, etc), so that I could do:

```js
var events = [];

Swal.on("show",() => events.push("show"));
Swal.on("hide",() => events.push("hide"));

// later, after the test finishes, remove consecutive duplicates
events = events.reduce((list,v) => (
    list.length == 0 || list[list.length - 1] != v ?
        [ ...list, v ] :
        list
),[]);

events;   // [ "show", "hide", "show", ... ]
```

Of course, the methods could be `on()` / `off()`, or `addListener()` / `removeListener()`, or whatever naming is preferred.

### New feature implementation

At a minimum, I think you'd need to add two methods (again, named `on()` / `off()`, etc) for registering an event listener, and removing an event listener. You *could* add more sugar helpers, like `removeAll()`, etc... but I think that's optional as a minimal feature set here.

There are several OSS libraries that already do event-emitter interfaces well, such as [EventEmitter3](https://github.com/primus/eventemitter3). I think you could find one of those and either bundle it in and use out-of-the-box, or at least pull a subset of its implementation from and drop-in to `Swal`'s definition.

Then in each place internally where Swal is doing things like adding `swal2-show` / `swal2-hide` classes, that's where you'd need to also have `emit(..)` calls to emit the proper events to the interface. The arguments to pass along should probably match/mirror the other internal event handlers (`willShow`, etc).

Also TBD would be which events need to be published on the emitter. I need "show" and "hide" minimally, but there may be others that are useful.

----

I'd be happy to try my hand at a PR, if this feature is considered something useful in scope for the project.

