Introduce basic support for push notifications mocking on Android
### Description

Define and implement an API for mocking push notification invocation events on Android, so as to introduce parity with the equivalent API for iOS.

This has long been a missing functionality on Android compared to iOS, as - unlike on iOS, there's no skeletal definition of notification invocation events. Rather, on Android, notifications fire pending intents, originally produced upon notification arrival handling *by the app itself*; All the while there's also no restriction as to how and when notifications show, and what the nature of the registered pending intent is.

The general direction is towards formalizing a reduced intents API, to be used by the JS test code.

#### Phase 1

In this basic-support phase, we limit the functionality to a naive one where the same intent is always used*, and the user is only able to provide payload, using the API's already defined for iOS.

This boils down to 3 types of use cases:

```js
    const userNotification = {
      payload: {
        userData1: 'userDataValue1',
        userData2: 'userDataValue2',
      },
    };

    // #1 - most common
    await device.launchApp({ newInstance: true, userNotification });

    // #2 - `launchApp` used for a running app (a not very appealing API overall)
    await device.launchApp({ newInstance: false, userNotification });

    // #3 - The dedicated function
    await device.launchApp({newInstance: true});
    await device.sendUserNotification(userNotification);
```
> Note: while `sendUserNotification()` is an API that's not very idiomatic on Android, we use it here nonetheless, assuming we will abandon all of this in phase 2 (see below).

#### Phase 2

For the full vision and more versatile Android-idiomatic solution, see #2141.

---
<sup> * This refers to a simply, launcher-like intent, with user payload "flat"-set in its extras -- much like the way the default FCM impl. handles *message* notifications on the device.</sup>

Consider whether to always use destructuring when extracting member expressions
Given a function for concatenating two arrays.

```javascript
export function concat(a1, a2) {
  const result = new Array(a1.length + a2.length)

  for (let i = 0; i < a1.length; i++) {
    result[i] = a1[i]
  }

  for (let i = 0; i < a2.length; i++) {
    result[i + a1.length] = a2[i]
  }

  return result
}
```

I'm repeating `a1.length` and `a2.length` a few times in the code, so I use "Extract variable" on both of them and select to replace all occurrences.

```javascript
export function concat(a1, a2) {
  const { length: a1Length } = a1
  const { length: a2Length } = a2
  const result = new Array(a1Length + a2Length)

  for (let i = 0; i < a1Length; i++) {
    result[i] = a1[i]
  }

  for (let i = 0; i < a2Length; i++) {
    result[i + a1Length] = a2[i]
  }

  return result
}
```

Since I'm extracting a property with the same name twice, I have to rename them. The result ends up being somewhat noisy, plus it is longer than the more "obvious" solution.

```javascript
export function concat(a1, a2) {
  const a1Length = a1.length
  const a2Length = a2.length
  const result = new Array(a1Length + a2Length)

  for (let i = 0; i < a1Length; i++) {
    result[i] = a1[i]
  }

  for (let i = 0; i < a2Length; i++) {
    result[i + a1Length] = a2[i]
  }

  return result
}
```

Perhaps it would be a good idea not to use destructuring if the extracted variable has a different name than the original property?

I suppose this might be difficult to do, since it looks like extraction is done before the new name has been decided. But YMMV.

## Additional information

- Version of the extension used: _v4.7.0_

Allow `*testing.T` before `context.Context` in the context parameter check
This issue mirrors the upstream one [here](https://github.com/golang/lint/issues/422), which ended up stalling out due to upstream abandoning golint.

The specific case I'm referring to looks like so:

```go
func TestFoo(t *testing.T) {
  ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(context.Backgrdoun())
  defer cancel()
  t.Run("subtest1", func(t *tesitng.T) {
    ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(ctx)
    fooSubtest1(t, ctx)
  })
}

func fooSubtest1(t *testing.T, ctx context.Context) {
  // do things that require a context
}
```

Currently, the `context-as-argument` lint will complain that `fooSubtest1` should be written with the signature `func(context.Context, *testing.T)` instead.

To me, it's idiomatic for `*testing.T` to remain the first argument of `fooSubtest` since it's effectively being curried in. Ideally, if this language supported currying, we'd do something more akin to `subtest1 := t.Run("subtest1", fooSubtest1); subtest1(ctx)`, but without language support for currying, it seems like manually passing through the `t` first makes more sense.

So, that leads me to the question for this issue:
Should we update revive to allow this? Is there some other hoops we should jump through first?

I'm happy to provide the code changes necessary to make the current context lint accept this (and have them written up [over here](https://github.com/euank/revive/commit/9a452fbac3ed67c59ca37fe73404b99729d192ba) in fact.)

